<article class="guide-content"><h1>Freelance Pricing: Billable Hours, Cash Reserves and Payment Gaps</h1>
<p class="byline">Econified · Updated September 5, 2026 · <a href="/how-it-works/">Methodology and corrections</a></p>

<p>A profitable contract can leave you short of cash before an invoice is paid. Calculate the revenue your hours must support, then test the cash you need between payments.</p>
<h2>Build a budget before choosing a premium</h2><p>List personal spending, business costs, replacement benefits and tax reserves. Keep each item on one line so it appears once. A flat percentage is a scenario allowance, not evidence of your actual insurance or tax costs.</p>
<p>For US contracting, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc554">IRS self-employment tax guidance</a> describes a calculation involving net earnings and specific rules. A budget allowance does not calculate that liability or replace income-tax planning.</p>
<h2>Divide by hours clients pay for</h2><p>Suppose a budget requires $60,000 annual revenue. At 25 billable hours a week for 46 weeks, you have 1,150 annual billable hours and need $52.17/hour. At 20 hours over those weeks, you need $65.22/hour. The requirement is unchanged; fewer paid hours support it.</p>
<p>Exclude sales, administration, unpaid leave and expected gaps from billable hours. If those hours are already excluded, do not add another percentage for the same missing hours. A contingency for unexpected expenses is a separate assumption.</p>
<h2>Distinguish markup from revenue deductions</h2><p>A 20% markup on a $60,000 budget produces $72,000. A 20% deduction from revenue requires $75,000 to leave $60,000. The formulas are $60,000 × 1.2 and $60,000 / 0.8. Confusing them understates the required rate.</p>
<p>The <a href="/freelance-rate-calculator/">freelance rate tool</a> adds a markup to a salary target. The <a href="/contractor-vs-employee/">contractor comparison</a> deducts overhead from revenue. Choose the interpretation matching your budget.</p>
<h2>Check cash until payment</h2><p>With $24,000 available and $4,000 monthly spending, no-revenue runway is six months. A $3,000 tax bill already due reduces cash to $21,000 and runway to 5.25 months. An unpaid invoice is not cash in the bank.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/an-essential-guide-to-building-an-emergency-fund/">CFPB's emergency-fund guidance</a> explains that reserve needs depend on your circumstances. No single reserve or rate guarantees safety.</p>
<p>Run the <a href="/freelance-risk-calculator/">runway and revenue buffer calculator</a> with fewer hours and higher spending. Separately write down invoice dates, payment terms and bills due before payment. That reveals timing problems an annual rate cannot capture.</p>

